        "start_index": 1892,
        "title": "Here\u2019s a look at the annual compensation planning process at Google. ",
        "type": "url_citation",
        "url": "https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf"
       },
       {
        "end_index": 2389,
        "start_index": 2276,
        "title": "Google Product Manager Salary in San Francisco Bay Area | $207K-$2.45M+ | Levels.fyi",
        "type": "url_citation",
        "url": "https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area"
       }
      ],
      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a strong L6 offer. I would negotiate once, professionally, and almost certainly accept rather than walk away.**\n\nThe offer is already above reported market medians, especially in equity and sign-on. Your negotiation should aim to improve the long-term package\u2014not communicate that the current offer is inadequate.\n\n## What the offer is actually worth\n\nYour guaranteed target cash compensation is:\n\n- Base: **$260,000**\n- Target bonus: **$52,000**\n- Annual cash: **$312,000**\n\nIf the equity were evenly distributed, the $1.34 million grant would add $335,000 annually:\n\n- Recurring four-year average: **$647,000/year**\n- Including the $200,000 sign-on, amortized over four years: **$697,000/year**\n\nThat compares favorably with your approximately **$526,000 current annual Meta compensation**.\n\nHowever, Google commonly front-loads new-hire grants at approximately **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**. Confirm the actual schedule in your offer documents; your individual award controls. ([thesimplemoney.com](https://thesimplemoney.com/employers/google/gsu-grants-and-vesting-schedule))\n\nIf that standard schedule applies and Alphabet\u2019s stock price stays constant:\n\n| Year | Base + target bonus | New-hire equity | Sign-on | Total |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | $312K | $509K | $200K | **$1.021M** |\n| 2 | $312K | $429K | \u2014 | **$741K** |\n| 3 | $312K | $268K | \u2014 | **$580K** |\n| 4 | $312K | $134K | \u2014 | **$446K** |\n\nThis reveals the main weakness: **the Year 4 cliff**. Refresh grants may mitigate it, but you should not treat unguaranteed refreshes as part of the offer.\n\nGoogle\u2019s own compensation materials describe equity refreshes as part of annual compensation planning, based on factors such as performance, location, role, manager discretion, and available discretionary budget. That supports the recruiter\u2019s characterization: refreshes are real, but not contractual. ([services.google.com](https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf))\n\n## Is it market competitive?\n\nYes\u2014more than merely competitive.\n\nRecent reported Bay Area data puts median Google L6 PM compensation around **$551,000**, although self-reported compensation sites have methodological limitations and mix employees at different points in their grant cycles. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area))\n\nYour package has:\n\n- Base at **96% of the posted $271,000 maximum**\n- A substantial **$1.34 million equity grant**\n- An unusually meaningful **$200,000 sign-on**\n- Approximately **$647,000 average recurring target compensation** before future refreshes\n- Approximately **$697,000 four-year average** including sign-on\n\nThe sign-on also effectively replaces the approximately $200,000 of Meta equity you would forfeit over the next 12 months. In fact, based on your forward vesting number, staying at Meta would give you roughly:\n\n- $240K base\n- $36K bonus\n- $200K next-12-month RSUs\n- **$476K forward 12-month compensation**\n\nSo Google is not asking you to take a near-term financial loss.\n\n## What I would negotiate\n\n### Opening request\n\nAsk for:\n\n- **Base: $271,000**\n- **New-hire equity: $1,550,000\u2013$1,600,000**\n- **Sign-on: $250,000**\n\nA clean specific ask would be:\n\n> **$271,000 base, $1.6 million in new-hire equity, and a $250,000 sign-on bonus.**\n\nThis is an anchor, not necessarily the expected landing point. It represents:\n\n- An 11K base increase, still within the published range\n- About a 19% equity increase\n- A 50K sign-on increase\n\nThat is assertive but not unreasonable for an experienced Meta IC6 whom Google actively recruited.\n\n### Realistic landing outcome\n\nI would consider any of these a good negotiated result:\n\n- Base increased to **$265K\u2013$271K**\n- Equity increased to **$1.45M\u2013$1.55M**\n- Sign-on kept at $200K or increased to **$225K\u2013$250K**\n\nIf Google will move only one component, prioritize:\n\n1. **Equity**\n2. **Base**\n3. **Sign-on**\n\nEquity is the largest lever, and increasing the initial grant helps with the later-year cliff. Base is permanent and affects target bonus, but there is only $11,000 of visible room in the posted range. Sign-on is valuable but one-time and may have a repayment obligation.\n\nFor example, moving equity from $1.34 million to $1.55 million adds $210,000 over four years. Under a 38/32/20/10 schedule, that is approximately:\n\n- Year 1: +$80K\n- Year 2: +$67K\n- Year 3: +$42K\n- Year 4: +$21K\n\nIt improves the package without radically changing its structure.\n\n## Suggested wording\n\n> I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and role, and Google is my first choice. After reviewing the package carefully, I\u2019d like to see whether there is room to improve it before I make my final decision.\n>\n> I\u2019m currently at approximately $526,000 in annual compensation at Meta, and I would be walking away from roughly $200,000 of equity vesting over the next 12 months. Given my current compensation, my experience operating at the IC6 level, and the scope of this L6 role, I was hoping we could get closer to a $271,000 base, $1.6 million in new-hire equity, and a $250,000 sign-on bonus.\n>\n> I recognize the current offer is substantial, and I\u2019m flexible about how the package is structured. The most important component to me is the equity grant, particularly given the later-year vesting decline. If you can improve the package meaningfully, I would be very enthusiastic about moving forward.\n\nIf you are genuinely prepared to sign immediately at a particular number, you can add:\n\n> If we can reach that package\u2014or get substantially closer on the equity\u2014I would be prepared to conclude the process promptly.\n\nDo **not** say \u201cI\u2019ll sign today\u201d unless you really will.\n\n## Other items to clarify before accepting\n\nAsk the recruiter to confirm in writing:\n\n1. **Exact vesting schedule:** Is it 38/32/20/10, and monthly or quarterly?\n2. **Equity conversion:** On what date and using what stock-price methodology is the $1.34 million converted into shares?\n3. **Sign-on payment timing:** First paycheck, first month, or another date?\n4. **Sign-on clawback:** How long is the repayment period, and is repayment prorated?\n5. **First refresh eligibility:** Which compensation cycle will you first participate in, and could your start date affect eligibility?\n6. **First bonus eligibility:** Is the first annual bonus prorated?\n7. **Start date flexibility:** If you have a meaningful Meta vest shortly before the proposed start date, consider requesting a later start rather than abandoning that vest.\n\n## When would walking away make sense?\n\nI would not walk away solely because of compensation. I would walk only if:\n\n- You require compensation to remain above $526K in **every** individual year without assuming refreshes\u2014because Year 4 does not;\n- The role, manager, product area, or promotion prospects are materially worse than staying at Meta;\n- The equity or sign-on terms contain unexpectedly unfavorable conditions;\n- Your Meta equity has unusually attractive upside that is not reflected in the $200K valuation.\n\nOtherwise, the rational sequence is:\n\n1. **Negotiate once**\n2. **Let Google respond**\n3. **Accept even if the improvement is modest\u2014or potentially if they hold firm**\n\nThe current offer is already strong enough that an unsuccessful negotiation should not, by itself, turn it into a rejection. The best framing is: **\u201cI want to join; help me close the remaining compensation gap,\u201d** not **\u201cmeet these numbers or I walk.\u201d**",
